Article 23 (Exchange of Operating Procedure Manuals, etc.)
(1) Railroad facility managers and railroad operators shall, pursuant to Article 17 (2) of the Act, prepare operating procedure manuals for fields requiring mutual cooperation in the management of railroad facilities and the operation of railroads, exchange them regularly, and give immediate notice of any changes.
(2) Railroad facility managers and railroad operators shall regularly conduct joint inspections in fields requiring mutual cooperation.

Article 24 (Establishment of Track Allocation Guidelines, Etc.)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall establish and publicly notify guidelines on the allocation of track capacity (hereinafter referred to as "track allocation guidelines") pursuant to Article 17(2) of the Act, to enable railroad facility managers and railroad operators to use tracks safely and efficiently. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(2) The track allocation guidelines under paragraph (1) shall include the following matters:
1. Allocation of track capacity to passenger trains and freight trains;
2. Allocation of track capacity to interregional trains and intraregional trains;
3. Working time for the maintenance and repair, improvement, and construction of tracks;
4. Matters concerning the safe operation of rolling stock;
5. Other matters necessary for the efficient use of tracks.
(3) Persons who manage or use tracks, including railroad facility managers and railroad operators, shall comply with the track allocation guidelines under paragraph (1).
(4) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall install and operate railroad traffic control facilities to enable measures necessary to maintain the safety and order of railroad traffic, such as providing information on the operation of rolling stock, etc., controlling the operation of rolling stock, etc., providing guidance and supervision on whether operations comply with the law, and issuing instructions for accident recovery when accidents occur. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 25 (Contents of the Master Plan for Railroad Industry Structural Reform)
"Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 18(2)7 of the Act means the following matters: <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
1. Matters concerning restructuring of the railroad service market;
2. Matters concerning pricing policies, including railroad fares and fees for the use of railroad facilities;
3. Matters concerning railroad safety and service improvement;
4. Matters concerning the system for implementing railroad industry structural reform and cooperation among relevant institutions;
5. Matters concerning the medium- and long-term direction for implementing railroad industry structural reform;
6. Other matters that the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems necessary for implementing railroad industry structural reform.

Article 26 (Minor Changes to the Master Plan for Railroad Industry Structural Reform)
"Minor changes prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the latter part of Article 18(3) of the Act means a change of not more than one year in the implementation period of the master plan for railroad industry structural reform.

Article 27 (Procedures for Establishing Implementation Plans for Railroad Industry Structural Reform, Etc.)
(1) The heads of relevant administrative agencies shall submit the implementation plans for the relevant year under Article 18(5) of the Act to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport by the end of November of the preceding year. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(2) The heads of relevant administrative agencies shall submit the implementation results of the preceding year's implementation plans to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport by the end of February each year. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 28 (Scope of Duties Performed on Behalf of the Managing Authority)
Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport has Korea National Railway act on the Minister's behalf pursuant to Article 19(2) of the Act, the duties to be performed on behalf of the Minister shall be as follows: <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013; September 10, 2020>
1. Implementation of railroad facility construction projects undertaken by the State;
2. Performance of management duties, including the collection of use fees for State-owned railroad facilities;
3. Duties prescribed by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for the safety of railroad facilities, such as maintaining the safety of railroad facilities and conducting comprehensive performance verification and safety condition inspections of railroad facilities in conjunction with the rolling stock using them;
4. Other duties that the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems necessary for the efficient management of railroad facilities.

Article 29 (Contents of Railroad Asset Disposal Plans)
A railroad asset disposal plan under Article 23(1) of the Act shall include the following matters: <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
1. Matters concerning an overview and the current status of railroad assets;
2. Matters concerning the direction for disposal of railroad assets;
3. Matters concerning the criteria for classifying railroad assets;
4. Matters concerning the handover, transfer, and contribution as capital of railroad assets;
5. Matters concerning the schedule for implementing the disposal of railroad assets;
6. Other matters that the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems necessary for the disposal of railroad assets.

Article 30 (Plans for Entrusting the Management of Railroad Assets to the Private Sector)
(1) "Private corporations prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the portion of Article 23(4) of the Act other than its subparagraphs means nonprofit corporations established under the Civil Act and stock companies established under the Commercial Act.
(2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport intends to entrust the management of railroad assets to a private corporation pursuant to Article 23(4) of the Act, the Minister shall establish a plan for entrustment to the private sector after deliberation by the Committee. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(3) A plan for entrustment to the private sector under paragraph (2) shall include the following matters:
1. Railroad assets subject to entrustment;
2. The necessity, scope, and effects of entrustment;
3. Procedures for selecting the entrusted entity.
(4) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport has established a plan for entrustment to the private sector pursuant to paragraph (2), the Minister shall publicly notify the plan. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 31 (Conclusion of Entrustment Contracts with the Private Sector)
(1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport intends to entrust the management of railroad assets pursuant to Article 23(4) of the Act, the Minister shall select a person deemed suitable to manage the relevant railroad assets from among those who have submitted business plans in accordance with the plan for entrustment to the private sector publicly notified under Article 30(4), and shall conclude an entrustment contract with that person. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(2) An entrustment contract under paragraph (1) shall include the following matters: <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
1. Railroad assets subject to entrustment;
2. Matters concerning the management of railroad assets subject to entrustment;
3. The term of the entrustment contract (including matters concerning modification and renewal of the contract term and termination of the entrustment contract);
4. Matters concerning payment of compensation for the entrusted duties;
5. Matters concerning management and supervision of the entrusted duties;
6. Matters concerning re-entrustment of the entrusted duties;
7. Other matters that the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems necessary.

Article 32 (Procedures and Timing for the Handover, Transfer, Etc. of Railroad Assets)
(1) Where the Administrator of the Korean National Railroad Administration or the Chairperson of the Korea High-Speed Rail Construction Authority intends to obtain approval for the handover, transfer, etc. of railroad assets pursuant to Article 23(6) of the Act, the Administrator or Chairperson shall submit to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport an application for approval stating the scope, list, and value of the assets to be handed over or transferred, together with the documents necessary for the handover or transfer. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(2) The timing for the handover, transfer, etc. of railroad assets under Article 23(7) of the Act shall be as follows: <Amended on February 29, 2008; September 10, 2020>
1. The date on which Korea Railroad Corporation receives railroad assets as a capital contribution under Article 23(2) of the Act: The date of registration of incorporation of Korea Railroad Corporation;
2. The date on which the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport receives the transfer of railroad assets under Article 23(4) of the Act: January 1, 2004;
3. The date on which Korea National Railway receives the handover of railroad assets under Article 23(5) of the Act: January 1, 2004.
(3) The valuation reference date for railroad assets subject to handover, transfer, etc. shall be the day preceding the date on which the assets are received by handover, transfer, etc. under paragraph (2): Provided, That the valuation reference date for railroad assets contributed as capital to Korea Railroad Corporation pursuant to Article 23(2) of the Act shall be as prescribed in the State Property Act. <Amended on July 27, 2009>
(4) The appraised value of railroad assets subject to handover, transfer, etc. shall be the book value of the assets as of the valuation reference date under paragraph (3): Provided, That the method of valuation of railroad assets contributed as capital to Korea Railroad Corporation pursuant to Article 23(2) of the Act shall be as prescribed in the State Property Act. <Amended on July 27, 2009>

Article 33 (Procedures and Timing for the Handover of Railroad Debts)
(1) Where the Administrator of the Korean National Railroad Administration or the Chairperson of the Korea High-Speed Rail Construction Authority intends to obtain approval for the handover of railroad debts pursuant to Article 24(3) of the Act, the Administrator or Chairperson shall submit to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport an application for approval stating the scope, list, and value of the debts to be handed over, together with the documents necessary for the handover. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(2) The timing for the handover of railroad debts under Article 24(4) of the Act shall be as follows: <Amended on September 10, 2020>
1. The date on which Korea Railroad Corporation takes over operating debts pursuant to Article 24(2) of the Act: The date of registration of incorporation of Korea Railroad Corporation;
2. The date on which Korea National Railway takes over facility debts pursuant to Article 24(2) of the Act: January 1, 2004;
3. The date on which the general account takes over other debts pursuant to Article 24(2) of the Act: January 1, 2004.
(3) The valuation reference date for railroad debts being handed over shall be the day preceding the date of handover under paragraph (2).
(4) The appraised value of railroad debts being handed over shall be the book value of the debts as of the valuation reference date.

Article 34 (Permission for Use of Railroad Facilities)
Matters concerning the criteria, methods, procedures, periods, etc. for permission by the managing authority under Article 31(1) of the Act shall be governed by the State Property Act.

Article 34-2 (Use Fees for Railroad Facilities under Permission for Use, Etc.)
(1) Where a person intending to use railroad facilities uses such facilities with permission from the managing authority pursuant to Article 31(1) of the Act, the use fees for the railroad facilities that the managing authority may collect pursuant to the main clause of paragraph (2) of the same Article shall be governed by Article 32 of the State Property Act.
(2) Where a local government intends to use railroad facilities directly for official use, public use, or nonprofit public interest projects, the managing authority may exempt it from use fees pursuant to the proviso to Article 31(2) of the Act in accordance with the criteria for the following categories:
1. Permission for use with a permitted use period of not more than one year, where use is sought on the condition of acquiring the railroad facilities: All of the use fees;
2. Permission for use other than the permission specified in subparagraph 1: 60/100 of the use fees.
(3) Except as provided in this Decree, matters concerning the criteria and procedures for collecting use fees for railroad facilities under permission for use shall be governed by the State Property Act.

Article 35 (Use Contracts for Railroad Facilities)
(1) A use contract for railroad facilities under Article 31(1) of the Act shall include the following matters: <Amended on July 4, 2022>
1. The period of use, facilities subject to use, conditions of use, and use fees;
2. The scope of, and conditions for, consent to third-party use of the facilities concerned;
3. Mutual responsibilities and measures to be taken in the event of a breach of contract;
4. Mediation procedures in the event of a dispute;
5. Measures to be taken in an emergency;
6. Matters concerning renewal of the contract;
7. Matters concerning the prohibition on disclosure of the contents of the contract.
(2) To conclude a use contract under Article 31(1) of the Act for the railroad facilities specified in Article 3, subparagraph 2 (a) through (d) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "tracks, etc.") (hereinafter referred to as a "use contract for tracks, etc."), all of the following criteria shall be met: <Amended on July 4, 2022>
1. The relevant tracks, etc. are intended to be used for passenger or freight transportation;
2. The period of use does not exceed five years.
(3) The conditions of use of tracks, etc. under paragraph (1)1 shall include the following matters and shall not conflict with the track allocation guidelines under Article 24(1): <Amended on July 4, 2022>
1. The types and lengths of rolling stock to be put into operation;
2. The daily frequency of operation, starting time, ending time, and operating intervals of rolling stock;
3. Originating stations, intermediate stops, and terminal stations;
4. Matters concerning the safety of railroad operations;
5. The level of railroad passenger or freight transportation services.
(4) Where a railroad facility manager intends to allow a person intending to use railroad facilities to use such facilities by concluding a use contract with that person pursuant to Article 31(1) of the Act, the railroad facility manager shall give public notice thereof in advance. <Amended on July 4, 2022>

Article 36 (Use Fees for Tracks, Etc. under Use Contracts, Etc.)
(1) When determining use fees for tracks, etc. under Article 35(1)1, a railroad facility manager shall allow for the recovery of relevant costs, including the maintenance and repair costs of tracks, etc., without exceeding the following limits: Provided, That a railroad facility manager that has been granted a right to manage and operate infrastructure pursuant to Article 26 of the Act on Public-Private Partnerships in Infrastructure shall determine use fees for tracks, etc. as prescribed in that Act. <Amended on March 8, 2005; July 4, 2022>
1. Tracks, etc. for which the State or a local government has borne the entire construction project costs: The total maintenance and repair costs of the relevant tracks, etc.;
2. Tracks, etc. other than those under subparagraph 1: The sum of the total maintenance and repair costs of the relevant tracks, etc. and the total construction project costs (meaning the amount remaining after deducting costs borne by the State or a local government, or by a beneficiary under Article 37(1) of the Act, from the aggregate of survey costs, design costs, construction costs, compensation costs, and other costs incurred in construction).
(2) When determining use fees for tracks, etc. pursuant to the main clause of paragraph (1), other than its subparagraphs, a railroad facility manager may consider the following matters: <Amended on July 4, 2022>
1. The condition of tracks, etc., including track grade and track capacity;
2. The types and weights of rolling stock in operation;
3. The operating time periods and frequency of operation of rolling stock;
4. The frequency and severity of railroad accidents;
5. The level of railroad services;
6. Efficiency and the public interest in railroad management.
(3) Deleted. <July 4, 2022>

Article 37 (Procedures for Concluding Use Contracts for Tracks, Etc.)
(1) A person who intends to conclude a use contract for tracks, etc. under Article 35(2) (hereinafter referred to as an "applicant for use") shall submit to the railroad facility manager an application for a use contract for tracks, etc. stating the purpose of use of the tracks, etc., together with the following documents:
1. Documents evidencing eligibility to engage in the railroad passenger or freight transportation business;
2. A business plan for railroad passenger or freight transportation;
3. Documents enabling confirmation of the specifications and safety of rolling stock and operating facilities.
(2) A railroad facility manager shall notify the applicant for use of the schedule for consultations on the conclusion of a use contract for tracks, etc. within one month from the date of receipt of the application for a use contract for tracks, etc. under paragraph (1).
(3) Where an applicant for use requests information on railroad facilities, the railroad facility manager shall comply with the request unless there is a special reason not to do so.
(4) Where a railroad facility manager intends to conclude a use contract for tracks, etc. with an applicant for use, the railroad facility manager shall obtain prior approval from the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. The same shall also apply to amendments to the terms of a use contract for tracks, etc. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 38 (Renewal of Use Contracts for Tracks, Etc.)
(1) A person who has concluded a use contract for tracks, etc. and is using the tracks, etc. (hereinafter referred to as a "party to a use contract for tracks, etc.") shall apply for renewal of the use contract for tracks, etc. no later than 10 months before the expiration of the period of use if the person intends to continue using the tracks, etc.
(2) Where a party to a use contract for tracks, etc. applies for renewal of the use contract for tracks, etc. pursuant to paragraph (1), the railroad facility manager shall give priority to consultations with that party on the use of the relevant tracks, etc., unless there are special grounds not to do so. In such cases, Article 35(4) shall not apply.
(3) Article 35(1) through (3), Articles 36 and 37 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the renewal of use contracts for tracks, etc.

Article 39 (Consent to Use Railroad Facilities)
(1) A person who has concluded a use contract for railroad facilities under Article 35 (1) (hereinafter referred to in this Article as a "facility use contracting party") may, pursuant to Article 31 (1) of the Act, consent to the use by a third party of part of the railroad facilities for which the use contract was concluded. In such cases, the person shall consult in advance with the railroad facility manager.
(2) Where a facility use contracting party consents to use by a third party under paragraph (1), the party shall notify the railroad facility manager of the details thereof.

Article 40 (Securing a Budget for Compensation for Public Service Costs)
(1) A railroad operator shall, by the end of March each year, submit to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport a statement of estimated State-borne costs specifying the estimated amount of public service costs to be borne by the State in the following year under Article 32 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "State-borne costs"), the details of the relevant public service, and other necessary matters. In such cases, when calculating the estimated amount of State-borne costs, the railroad operator shall take into consideration compensation contracts under Article 33 (1) of the Act, etc. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(2) Upon receipt of a statement of estimated State-borne costs under paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall, in consultation with the heads of the relevant administrative agencies, include the State-borne costs in the General Account under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport for the following year. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(3) When determining State-borne costs under paragraph (2), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall take into consideration the estimated amount of State-borne costs under paragraph (1), the State-borne costs borne in the preceding year, the provisions of relevant statutes and regulations, or compensation contracts under Article 33 (1) of the Act, etc. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 41 (Payment of State-Borne Costs)
(1) A railroad operator intending to apply for payment of State-borne costs shall submit an application for payment of State-borne costs to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport within the period designated by the Minister, accompanied by the following documents: <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
1. The amount of State-borne costs for which payment is requested and a statement of the calculation details;
2. A statement of anticipated revenues and expenditures for the relevant year;
3. A statement of State-borne costs received during the preceding two years;
4. A cost calculation statement.
(2) Upon receipt of an application for payment of State-borne costs under paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall examine the application and pay the State-borne costs at the beginning of each half-year. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 42 (Settlement of State-Borne Costs)
(1) A railroad operator that has received payment of State-borne costs under Article 41 (2) shall, within 30 days after the end of the relevant half-year, submit to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport a statement of settlement of State-borne costs, accompanied by the following documents: <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
1. A statement of revenues and expenditures;
2. Supporting documents for revenues and expenditures;
3. Other accounting documents, such as cash flow statements.
(2) Upon receipt of a statement of settlement of State-borne costs under paragraph (1), the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may have the specialized institutions, etc. referred to in Article 33 (4) of the Act verify it. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 43 (Separation of Accounts, Etc.)
(1) A railroad operator receiving payment of State-borne costs shall keep the accounts for the lines and stations referred to in Article 32 (2) 2 of the Act separate from other accounts.
(2) The fiscal year of a railroad operator receiving payment of State-borne costs shall be the same as the Government's fiscal year.

Article 44 (Documents to Accompany an Application for Approval of Closure of Specific Lines, Etc.)
When a railroad facility manager and a railroad operator submit an application for approval to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport under Article 34 (2) of the Act, they shall attach documents stating the following matters: <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
1. The reasons for the application for approval;
2. The status of operations, including the frequency of operation of rolling stock by class and time slot, the number of stations, and the number of employees;
3. Average daily demand for railroad services for at least the preceding six months;
4. An accounting report on revenues, costs, and operating losses for at least the preceding year;
5. A forecast of average daily demand for railroad services over the next five years;
6. The total amount of public service costs over the preceding five years and the amount of public service costs borne by the party giving rise to the costs under Article 32 (1) of the Act;
7. The availability of alternative means of transportation.

Article 45 (Survey of Actual Conditions)
(1) Upon receipt of an application for approval under Article 34 (2) of the Act, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall conduct a survey of actual conditions concerning the status of operation of the relevant lines and stations or the status of provision of railroad services. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(2) If necessary, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may have the relevant local governments or specialized institutions participate in the survey of actual conditions under paragraph (1). <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall report the results of the survey of actual conditions under paragraph (1) to the Committee. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 46 (Public Notice of Closure of Specific Lines, Etc.)
Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport grants approval under Article 34 (3) of the Act, the Minister shall, within one month from the date of approval, give public notice of the specific lines and stations to be closed, or the details of the railroad services to be restricted or suspended, and the reasons therefor, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 47 (Formulation of Transportation Measures Following Closure of Specific Lines, Etc.)
The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the heads of the relevant administrative agencies shall, pursuant to Article 34 (4) of the Act, formulate and implement transportation measures that include the following matters for areas where transportation services would be seriously disrupted due to the absence or marked insufficiency of alternative means of transportation, among the areas affected by such measures as the closure of specific lines and stations or the restriction or suspension of railroad services: <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
1. Analysis of transportation conditions;
2. Increasing the frequency of service of alternative means of transportation, adjusting their routes, or deploying additional such means;
3. Securing funding necessary for alternative transportation;
4. Other matters necessary for the efficient implementation of transportation measures.

Article 48 (Selection of a New Operator Following Restriction or Suspension of Railroad Services)
(1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport deems it appropriate to have a new railroad operator (hereinafter referred to in this Article as a "new operator") provide, for specific lines and stations, the railroad services which an applicant for approval who is a railroad operator (hereinafter referred to in this Article as an "existing operator") intends to restrict or suspend under Article 34 (1) of the Act, the Minister may select a new operator pursuant to Article 34 (4) of the Act. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport intends to select a new operator under paragraph (1), the Minister shall, in consultation with the party giving rise to the costs under Article 32 (1) of the Act, select the new operator through competition. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(3) The party giving rise to the costs shall conclude a compensation contract under Article 33 of the Act with the new operator, and the existing operator shall prepare handover documents concerning the relevant railroad services, etc. and provide them to the new operator.
(4) Detailed methods for selecting a new operator, handover procedures, and other necessary matters under paragraphs (2) and (3) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>

Article 49 (Dispositions in Emergencies)
"Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 36 (1) 7 of the Act means the following matters:
1. Temporary use of railroad facilities;
2. Restrictions on the use of railroad facilities and control of access thereto;
3. Emergency restoration of railroad facilities and support for restoration;
4. Searches of railroad stations and rolling stock, etc.

Article 50 (Entrustment of Authority)
(1) Pursuant to the main clause of Article 38 of the Act, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall entrust the duties of establishing and operating the Railroad Industry Information Center under Article 12 (2) of the Act to a person prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport from among the following persons: <Amended on December 3, 2004; February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013; September 10, 2020>
1. A government-funded research institute under the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes or the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Science and Technology Research Institutes;
2. Korea National Railway.
(2) Pursuant to the main clause of Article 38 of the Act, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall entrust the work of carrying out maintenance and repair of railroad facilities to the Administrator of the Korean National Railroad Administration. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(3) Pursuant to the main clause of Article 38 of the Act, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall entrust the duties of managing railroad traffic control facilities and controlling railroad traffic under Article 24 (4) to a person prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport from among the following persons: <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013; September 10, 2020>
1. Korea National Railway;
2. A railroad operator.

Article 51 (Administrative Fines)
(1) When the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport imposes an administrative fine under Article 42 (2) of the Act, the Minister shall, after investigating and confirming the relevant violation, notify the person subject to the administrative fine to pay it, specifying in writing the facts of the violation, the amount of the administrative fine, the method and period for filing an objection, and other relevant matters. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(2) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport intends to impose an administrative fine under paragraph (1), the Minister shall set a period of at least 10 days and give the person subject to the administrative fine an opportunity to state an opinion orally or in writing. In such cases, if no opinion is stated by the designated date, the person shall be deemed to have no opinion. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(3) When determining the amount of an administrative fine, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall take into account the motive for, degree of, and frequency of the relevant violation, etc. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>
(4) Procedures for collecting administrative fines shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport. <Amended on February 29, 2008; March 23, 2013>

